go vet complaining about loop variable being captured by func literal

<p>One of the common newcomer mistakes documented in go github's <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/wiki/CommonMistakes#using-goroutines-on-loop-iterator-variables" rel="nofollow noreferrer">page</a>, is neglecting to pass a loop variable as parameter to the goroutine (in which case the program's output could be inconsistent given that:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>there is a very good chance that when you run this code you will see
  the last element printed for every iteration instead of each value in
  sequence, because the goroutines will probably not begin executing
  until after the loop.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>So the proposed solution is (as said) to pass the actual loop var as goroutine parameter:</p>

<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>for _, val := range values {
    go func(val interface{}) {
        fmt.Println(val)
    }(val)
}

</code></pre>

<p>So why is my <code>go-vet</code> throwing to me </p>

<blockquote>
  <p>loop variable i captured by func literal</p>
</blockquote>

<p>in the following code?</p>

<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>func main() {
    ch := make(chan int)

    for i := 0; i &lt; 2; i++ {
        go func(emp int) {
            for range ch {
                rcv := &lt;-ch
                fmt.Printf("employee %d received signal %d\n", i, rcv)
            }
        }(i)
    }

}
</code></pre>

