Why is GCC warning me about a useless cast when using an inherited constructor?

<p>Consider the following C++ code:</p>

<pre><code>#include &lt;string&gt;

struct A {
    A(const std::string&amp; s): s(s) {}
    std::string s;
};

struct B: A {
    using A::A;
};

int main() {
    B b("test");
}
</code></pre>

<p>When I compile it via GCC 6.2.1 with the <code>-Wuseless-cast</code> parameter</p>

<pre><code>g++ -std=c++14 -Wuseless-cast test.cpp -o test
</code></pre>

<p>it emits the following warning:</p>

<pre><code>test.cpp: In constructor ‘B::B(const string&amp;)’:
test.cpp:9:14: warning: useless cast to type ‘const string&amp; {aka const std::__cxx11::basic_string&lt;char&gt;&amp;}’ [-Wuseless-cast]
    using A::A;
             ^
test.cpp: In function ‘int main()’:
test.cpp:13:15: note: synthesized method ‘B::B(const string&amp;)’ first required here
    B b("test");
              ^
</code></pre>

